<p class="_kicker_y96l8_118">Independent framework</p><h1>Global Peace &amp; Human Security Framework</h1><p class="_subtitle_y96l8_138">A framework for human dignity, peace, civilian protection, accountability, and long-term human security.</p><div class="_documentMeta_y96l8_147"><span>Release candidate RC-1</span><span>29 September 2026</span><span>English canonical edition</span><span>Voluntary framework</span><button type="button" class="_printButton_y96l8_397">Save or print as PDF</button></div><div class="_controlBlock_y96l8_159" aria-label="Document control"><div><span>Document class</span><strong>Independent public-interest framework</strong></div><div><span>Status</span><strong>Release candidate RC-1</strong></div><div><span>Issue date</span><strong>29 September 2026</strong></div><div><span>Authority</span><strong>Voluntary; non-governmental</strong></div><div><span>Revision basis</span><strong>Evidence, review, and documented amendment</strong></div></div><p class="_disclaimer_y96l8_189">This document is not international law, a treaty, a government program, a political platform, a military doctrine, or a diplomatic mandate.</p></section><section id="preamble" class="_article_y96l8_119"><p class="_articleLabel_y96l8_119">Preamble</p><h2>Shared human principles, locally applied.</h2><p class="_lead_y96l8_220">We affirm that every human life possesses equal moral worth.</p><p>Peace and human security require more than the absence of war. They require conditions in which people may live with dignity, safety, autonomy, opportunity, justice, belonging, meaningful participation, and hope for the future.</p><p>The Global Peace &amp; Human Security Framework establishes a common ethical foundation for reducing violence, protecting civilians, resolving conflict peacefully, strengthening communities, and supporting durable human security.</p><p>It is global in principle and locally led in practice. It does not ask communities to become the same. It establishes a shared human floor while preserving legitimate cultural, civic, legal, and institutional self-determination. It favors cooperation over domination, evidence over propaganda, shared prosperity over preventable deprivation, and human development over systems that treat people merely as means.</p></section><section id="article-i" class="_article_y96l8_119"><p class="_articleLabel_y96l8_119">Article I</p><h2>Purpose</h2><p>The purpose of this Framework is to provide a practical and principled structure for:</p><ol class="_romanList_y96l8_227"><li>Protecting human life, dignity, and civilian safety.</li><li>Preventing violence and reducing conditions that contribute to conflict.</li><li>Strengthening peaceful dialogue, mediation, reconciliation, and nonviolent problem-solving.</li><li>Preserving legitimate individual and community autonomy.</li><li>Supporting locally led solutions informed by culture, history, law, and lived experience.</li><li>Ensuring universal inclusion and meaningful participation, with accessible processes and added safeguards where barriers prevent people or communities from being heard.</li><li>Promoting evidence-based decision-making, transparency, and accountability.</li><li>Expanding the social and economic conditions that support durable peace, meaningful opportunity, and shared prosperity.</li><li>Encouraging cooperative relationships based on consent, reciprocity, mutual benefit, and respect for legitimate self-determination.</li><li>Ensuring that technology serves humanity while remaining subject to appropriate ethical safeguards.</li><li>Protecting the interests of future generations and the natural systems upon which human life depends.</li></ol></section><section id="article-ii" class="_article_y96l8_119"><p class="_articleLabel_y96l8_119">Article II</p><h2>Foundational principles</h2><div class="_principles_y96l8_245"><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">1</span><div><h3>Equal human worth</h3><p>Every person possesses equal moral worth and should be treated with dignity, regardless of identity, status, origin, belief, ability, or circumstance.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">2</span><div><h3>Human dignity and development</h3><p>Peace should protect not only survival, but the conditions people need to learn, grow, create, contribute, form relationships, and pursue meaningful lives.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">3</span><div><h3>Universal inclusion and participation</h3><p>Every person and community should have a meaningful opportunity to be heard, represented, and involved in decisions that affect them. Participation should be accessible, fair, and open to all, with additional safeguards where exclusion, discrimination, disability, displacement, poverty, age, language, geography, or other barriers limit equal participation.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">4</span><div><h3>Nonviolence and prevention</h3><p>Conflict should, wherever reasonably possible, be prevented, de-escalated, mediated, and resolved without violence.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">5</span><div><h3>Civilian protection</h3><p>The safety and dignity of civilians should remain central in situations of conflict, instability, humanitarian response, reconstruction, and recovery.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">6</span><div><h3>Autonomy and local leadership</h3><p>Individuals and communities should retain meaningful agency over decisions that affect their lives, culture, institutions, and future.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">7</span><div><h3>Reason, evidence, and truth</h3><p>Decisions should be informed by credible evidence, scientific inquiry, transparent reasoning, measurable outcomes, and willingness to correct error.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">8</span><div><h3>Shared prosperity</h3><p>Durable peace is strengthened when security, opportunity, education, health, economic participation, and the benefits of progress are broadly shared.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">9</span><div><h3>Cooperation and ethical partnership</h3><p>Relationships among communities, institutions, and nations should favor consent, reciprocity, mutual benefit, and problem-solving over domination or exploitation.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">10</span><div><h3>Accountability</h3><p>Where authority is exercised, it should be accompanied by responsibility, transparency, oversight, conflict-of-interest safeguards, and mechanisms for correction.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">11</span><div><h3>Responsible technology</h3><p>Technology should expand human capability and well-being while remaining subject to human oversight, proportionality, privacy, safety, and rights protections.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">12</span><div><h3>Long-term stewardship</h3><p>Present decisions should consider their effects on future generations, communities, institutions, natural resources, and the living world.</p></div></div></div></section><section id="article-iii" class="_article_y96l8_119"><p class="_articleLabel_y96l8_119">Article III</p><h2>Method of application</h2><p class="_methodLine_y96l8_280">Listen → Assess → Co-design → Act → Verify → Learn</p><div class="_methodGrid_y96l8_292"><div class="_methodItem_y96l8_266"><span>01</span><h3>Listen</h3><p>Understand the people, history, culture, risks, needs, and perspectives involved.</p></div><div class="_methodItem_y96l8_266"><span>02</span><h3>Assess</h3><p>Examine evidence, potential harms, competing interests, legal considerations, and practical constraints.</p></div><div class="_methodItem_y96l8_266"><span>03</span><h3>Co-design</h3><p>Develop solutions with meaningful participation from the people and communities affected.</p></div><div class="_methodItem_y96l8_266"><span>04</span><h3>Act</h3><p>Where action is taken under the principles of this Framework, implement proportionate, lawful, ethical, and practical measures intended to reduce harm and improve human security.</p></div><div class="_methodItem_y96l8_266"><span>05</span><h3>Verify</h3><p>Measure outcomes, identify unintended consequences, disclose relevant failures, and determine whether intended effects are occurring.</p></div><div class="_methodItem_y96l8_266"><span>06</span><h3>Learn</h3><p>Preserve what works, correct what does not, and improve future decisions.</p></div></div></section><section id="article-iv" class="_article_y96l8_119"><p class="_articleLabel_y96l8_119">Article IV</p><h2>Safeguards</h2><p>No action taken under the principles of this Framework should intentionally, or through reckless disregard of foreseeable harm:</p><ul class="_bullets_y96l8_228"><li>Target civilians.</li><li>Encourage political manipulation or voter coercion.</li><li>Fabricate evidence or knowingly spread false information.</li><li>Exploit vulnerable populations.</li><li>Remove legitimate community participation without compelling justification.</li><li>Use artificial intelligence to make autonomous high-stakes decisions affecting human rights, liberty, safety, or legal status.</li><li>Treat technological capability as sufficient justification for its use.</li><li>Conceal material conflicts of interest.</li><li>Misrepresent experimental programs as proven solutions.</li></ul><p class="_note_y96l8_317">Human judgment, accountability, proportionality, transparency, and appropriate professional oversight remain essential.</p></section><section id="article-v" class="_article_y96l8_119"><p class="_articleLabel_y96l8_119">Article V</p><h2>Status and limitations</h2><p>The Global Peace &amp; Human Security Framework is an independent public-interest framework currently undergoing development and review.</p><div class="_notBox_y96l8_318"><strong>It is not:</strong><p>International law; a treaty; a government program; a political party or political platform; a military doctrine; a diplomatic mandate; or a substitute for courts, governments, qualified mediators, humanitarian organizations, medical professionals, or other appropriately authorized institutions.</p></div><p>Adoption is voluntary unless its principles are separately incorporated into lawful agreements, institutional policies, or other formally authorized mechanisms.</p></section><section id="article-vi" class="_article_y96l8_119"><p class="_articleLabel_y96l8_119">Article VI</p><h2>Global application and local ownership</h2><p>The principles of the Framework are intended to be understandable and applicable across cultures and regions while respecting legitimate local self-determination.</p><p>English serves as the canonical reference edition for version control. Future language editions should undergo qualified cultural and linguistic review to preserve meaning rather than relying solely on literal machine translation.</p><p>The long-term objective is broad accessibility through written, audio, visual, plain-language, signed-language, and other appropriate formats.</p></section><section id="article-vii" class="_article_y96l8_119"><p class="_articleLabel_y96l8_119">Article VII</p><h2>Human development and shared future</h2><p>The Framework proceeds from the view that peace is not merely the management of conflict. It is the continuing development of conditions in which people can live with dignity, exercise meaningful agency, learn, create, cooperate across differences, and contribute to a shared future.</p><div class="_principles_y96l8_245"><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">A</span><div><h3>Human beings before systems</h3><p>Institutions, economies and technologies should be judged in part by how well they protect dignity, agency, safety and human development.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">B</span><div><h3>One humanity without forced sameness</h3><p>Universal human worth is compatible with cultural, national, religious, philosophical and local difference. Cooperation does not require uniformity.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">C</span><div><h3>Reason joined with compassion</h3><p>Evidence, science and critical inquiry should guide decisions, while empathy and lived experience help identify harms, needs and consequences that abstract measures can miss.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">D</span><div><h3>Progress measured by human capability</h3><p>Material progress has value when it expands people&#x27;s real ability to live securely, participate meaningfully, pursue knowledge, form relationships and shape their futures.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">E</span><div><h3>Technology in service to humanity</h3><p>Technical capability alone does not justify use. Technology should remain accountable to human rights, safety, privacy, proportionality and meaningful human control.</p></div></div></div></section><section id="article-viii" class="_article_y96l8_119"><p class="_articleLabel_y96l8_119">Article VIII</p><h2>Human security</h2><p>Human security concerns the practical conditions that allow people and communities to live free from severe and avoidable threats to life, dignity, livelihood, health, participation, and future opportunity. The Framework treats these conditions as interconnected rather than isolated.</p><div class="_principles_y96l8_245"><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">01</span><div><h3>Personal and community security</h3><p>Protection from violence, coercion, exploitation, abuse, organized crime, arbitrary harm, and other serious threats to personal and community safety.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">02</span><div><h3>Economic and livelihood security</h3><p>Reasonable opportunity to secure basic needs, participate in economic life, withstand shocks, and pursue a stable livelihood without exploitation or preventable exclusion.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">03</span><div><h3>Food, water, health, and shelter</h3><p>Access to the essential conditions required for survival, health, recovery, and human development, consistent with applicable rights and local circumstances.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">04</span><div><h3>Environmental and resource security</h3><p>Responsible stewardship of land, water, ecosystems, climate adaptation, and shared resources, including early attention to disputes that may intensify social or political tensions.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">05</span><div><h3>Civic and information security</h3><p>Meaningful participation, access to reliable information, freedom from manipulation and intimidation, and institutions capable of resolving disputes through peaceful and lawful means.</p></div></div></div></section><section id="article-ix" class="_article_y96l8_119"><p class="_articleLabel_y96l8_119">Article IX</p><h2>Prevention, peacebuilding, and recovery</h2><p>The Framework gives priority to prevention. Where violence has occurred, the objective is not only to stop immediate harm but also to reduce the conditions that make recurrence more likely.</p><ol class="_romanList_y96l8_227"><li>Identify emerging risks early, including exclusion, insecurity, institutional breakdown, resource pressures, displacement, severe misinformation, and other context-specific warning signs.</li><li>Use dialogue, mediation, negotiation, and other peaceful means first. This Framework does not direct, authorize, or endorse coercive or forceful measures; any such decision rests solely with lawfully authorized institutions acting under applicable law.</li><li>Encourage preparedness, informed consent, impartial process design, universal participation, local ownership, coordination, and attention to applicable law in mediation and peace processes.</li><li>Protect civilians and humanitarian access throughout conflict prevention, active conflict, ceasefire, transition, and recovery.</li><li>Where armed mobilization has occurred, support context-appropriate professional standards for disarmament, demobilization, reintegration, and community safety, without directing or authorizing such processes.</li><li>Support truth, accountability, repair, reconciliation, restorative measures, and transitional justice where appropriate, lawful, safe, and locally legitimate.</li><li>Rebuild legitimate institutions, public services, livelihoods, trust, and peaceful mechanisms for resolving disputes.</li><li>Monitor implementation after agreements are signed and respond to signs of exclusion, non-compliance, renewed violence, or unintended harm.</li></ol></section><section id="article-x" class="_article_y96l8_119"><p class="_articleLabel_y96l8_119">Article X</p><h2>Governance, justice, and accountability</h2><p>Durable peace depends on institutions that can exercise authority lawfully, fairly, transparently, and with meaningful limits. The Framework does not prescribe a single political system, constitutional form, or cultural model.</p><div class="_principles_y96l8_245"><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">A</span><div><h3>Legitimate and limited authority</h3><p>Public power should be exercised under known rules, subject to appropriate checks, review, and accountability, and not solely for the benefit of those who hold power.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">B</span><div><h3>Access to peaceful remedy</h3><p>People and communities should have practical, safe, and credible ways to raise grievances, seek remedy, contest decisions, and resolve disputes without violence.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">C</span><div><h3>Institutional integrity</h3><p>Conflict-of-interest controls, anti-corruption safeguards, transparent decision processes, independent scrutiny, and correction mechanisms should be proportionate to the authority being exercised.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">D</span><div><h3>Justice with prevention of recurrence</h3><p>Accountability should protect rights and public safety while also considering truth, repair, rehabilitation, reconciliation, and prevention of future harm where these approaches are lawful and appropriate.</p></div></div></div></section><section id="article-xi" class="_article_y96l8_119"><p class="_articleLabel_y96l8_119">Article XI</p><h2>Evidence, risk, and early warning</h2><p>Decisions made under this Framework should distinguish clearly between verified fact, reasonable inference, uncertainty, allegation, value judgment, and prediction. No single index, dataset, institution, or model should be treated as a complete representation of a society or conflict.</p><ol class="_romanList_y96l8_227"><li>Use multiple independent sources for consequential claims whenever feasible.</li><li>State relevant geography, population, time period, definitions, and known data limitations.</li><li>Combine quantitative evidence with local knowledge, qualitative evidence, and lived experience.</li><li>Assess political, security, economic, social, environmental, health, resource, and institutional risks together where they interact.</li><li>Do not convert early-warning indicators into automatic accusations, sanctions, targeting, or coercive action.</li><li>Review forecasts and risk models for bias, false confidence, feedback effects, and harm caused by misclassification.</li><li>Revise conclusions when stronger evidence or legitimate contextual knowledge becomes available.</li></ol></section><section id="article-xii" class="_article_y96l8_119"><p class="_articleLabel_y96l8_119">Article XII</p><h2>Technology and information integrity</h2><p>Technology and communication systems can strengthen peace, access, coordination, education, and accountability, but can also amplify surveillance, manipulation, exclusion, hate, fabricated evidence, and violence.</p><ol class="_romanList_y96l8_227"><li>Preserve meaningful human control over decisions affecting rights, liberty, physical safety, legal status, or access to essential services.</li><li>Protect privacy, security, consent, and data minimization in information systems.</li><li>Do not use artificial intelligence or automated systems to determine who is an enemy, select targets, manipulate political participation, fabricate evidence, or make autonomous lethal or coercive decisions.</li><li>Support reliable information, media and information literacy, transparent correction, and dialogue while respecting lawful freedom of expression.</li><li>Assess whether digital systems unintentionally exclude people because of language, disability, connectivity, literacy, geography, cost, or other barriers.</li><li>Apply higher levels of testing, review, auditability, and oversight as potential harm increases.</li></ol></section><section id="article-xiii" class="_article_y96l8_119"><p class="_articleLabel_y96l8_119">Article XIII</p><h2>Review, amendment, and version control</h2><p>This Framework is intended to remain stable in principle while remaining capable of correction and improvement. Changes should be documented rather than silently replacing prior guidance.</p><ol class="_romanList_y96l8_227"><li>Maintain one canonical edition for version control and archive superseded editions.</li><li>Record substantive amendments, their reasons, supporting evidence, and date of adoption.</li><li>Subject major revisions to independent legal, safeguarding, accessibility, cultural, technical, and subject-matter review appropriate to the change.</li><li>Provide a clear correction mechanism for factual errors, harmful ambiguity, or defective guidance.</li><li>Suspend or withdraw guidance when credible evidence shows that its continued use creates unacceptable risk.</li><li>Preserve culturally reviewed translations as controlled editions linked to the canonical text.</li><li>Do not represent draft, experimental, or unreviewed material as settled international consensus.</li></ol><div class="_note_y96l8_317"><strong>Corrections and safeguarding concerns</strong><p>A public channel for factual corrections, harmful ambiguity, and safeguarding concerns will be listed here before the Framework is promoted beyond the release-candidate stage.</p></div><h3 class="_subhead_y96l8_390">Revision record</h3><div class="_controlBlock_y96l8_159" aria-label="Revision record"><div><span>RC-1 · 29 Sep 2026</span><strong>Initial release candidate: Articles I–XV, Appendices A and B, and Declaration.</strong></div><div><span>RC-1 editorial revision · 29 Sep 2026</span><strong>Aligned contents with article headings; extended Article IV to reckless disregard of foreseeable harm; replaced directive wording with voluntary wording (Articles II, IX, XII); clarified that the Framework does not authorize coercive measures; added correction notice and this record.</strong></div><div><span>RC-1 independent-review reconciliation · 29 Sep 2026</span><strong>Softened residual directive language in Principle 10; clarified that the application method applies only where action is voluntarily taken under the Framework; strengthened the non-operational boundary around disarmament, demobilization, reintegration, and community safety. The corrections and safeguarding placeholder remains pending an operational public channel.</strong></div></div></section><section id="article-xiv" class="_article_y96l8_119"><p class="_articleLabel_y96l8_119">Article XIV</p><h2>Implementation and evaluation</h2><p>Application of this Framework should be proportionate to context, lawful authority, available evidence, potential harm, and the capacity of the people and institutions involved. No implementation model is presumed to fit every society or situation.</p><ol class="_romanList_y96l8_227"><li>Define the problem, affected population, geographic scope, legal setting, and decision authority before intervention.</li><li>Establish a documented baseline using available quantitative evidence, qualitative evidence, local knowledge, and relevant historical context.</li><li>Identify who may benefit, who may bear risk, who may be excluded, and whose consent or participation is required.</li><li>Set clear, measurable objectives while recognizing that not every important human outcome can be reduced to a single metric.</li><li>Use the least harmful and least coercive effective approach reasonably available.</li><li>Test uncertain approaches at an appropriately limited scale before wider adoption when the risks of failure are material.</li><li>Monitor intended effects, unintended effects, distributional impacts, public trust, accessibility, and indicators of renewed harm.</li><li>Provide accessible channels for complaint, correction, appeal, and community feedback.</li><li>Use independent or external review when consequences are high, interests are conflicted, or local confidence requires additional scrutiny.</li><li>Define stop, pause, correction, and exit conditions before or during implementation rather than only after failure occurs.</li><li>Publish or preserve a proportionate record of methods, evidence, decisions, limitations, outcomes, and lessons learned.</li></ol></section><section id="article-xv" class="_article_y96l8_119"><p class="_articleLabel_y96l8_119">Article XV</p><h2>Definitions and interpretation</h2><p>Terms in this Framework should be interpreted in their ordinary, good-faith meaning unless a more specific definition is provided below or required by applicable law. Definitions are intended to support clarity, not to override lawful local usage.</p><div class="_principles_y96l8_245"><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">01</span><div><h3>Human security</h3><p>The protection and strengthening of the conditions people need to live with safety, dignity, livelihood, health, agency, participation, and reasonable opportunity.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">02</span><div><h3>Universal participation</h3><p>A meaningful opportunity for every person and community affected by a decision to be heard or represented through fair and accessible processes, with additional safeguards where barriers prevent equal participation.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">03</span><div><h3>Local ownership</h3><p>Meaningful leadership, agency, and decision influence by the people, communities, and legitimate institutions most directly affected by a policy, process, or intervention.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">04</span><div><h3>Civilian protection</h3><p>Measures intended to prevent, reduce, or respond to threats of violence, coercion, abuse, deprivation, or other serious harm to persons not directly participating in hostilities, consistent with applicable law.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">05</span><div><h3>Evidence</h3><p>Information that can reasonably inform a conclusion, including verified data, documented observation, credible research, local knowledge, and lived experience, assessed according to source quality and context.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">06</span><div><h3>Safeguarding</h3><p>Measures designed to prevent exploitation, abuse, discrimination, retaliation, avoidable exposure to harm, or misuse of authority in activities carried out under or inspired by the Framework.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">07</span><div><h3>Proportionality</h3><p>The principle that the scale, intrusiveness, cost, and risk of an action should be reasonably related to the seriousness of the problem and the expected public benefit.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">08</span><div><h3>Meaningful human control</h3><p>Human responsibility and practical ability to understand, supervise, challenge, modify, pause, or stop consequential uses of technology rather than merely approving automated outcomes after the fact.</p></div></div></div></section><section id="appendix-a" class="_article_y96l8_119"><p class="_articleLabel_y96l8_119">Appendix A</p><h2>Research and historical foundation</h2><p>The Framework is informed by established international norms, peace and human-security practice, empirical research, historical experience, and a range of philosophical and cultural traditions. These sources do not confer legal authority on this Framework and are not treated as interchangeable.</p><div class="_principles_y96l8_245"><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">01</span><div><h3>Rights, law and civilian protection</h3><p>UN Charter; Universal Declaration of Human Rights; international humanitarian law and the Geneva Conventions tradition; Nuremberg Principles; disability rights; Indigenous rights; and related human-rights standards.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">02</span><div><h3>Human security and sustaining peace</h3><p>UNDP human security; the UN common understanding of human security; UNESCO, ILO and WHO constitutional principles; SDG 16; Women, Peace and Security; Youth, Peace and Security; and the UN–World Bank Pathways for Peace research.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">03</span><div><h3>Evidence and measurement</h3><p>Conflict and violence data from UCDP, ACLED and PRIO; arms and military-expenditure data from SIPRI; displacement data from UNHCR; human-development data from UNDP; governance and development data from the World Bank and UNDP; and food, health and child-well-being data from relevant UN agencies.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">04</span><div><h3>Historical and philosophical lineage</h3><p>Cosmopolitan traditions, Kant&#x27;s work on perpetual peace, traditions of nonviolence and reconciliation, restorative approaches to justice, and cross-cultural ideas of reciprocity, hospitality, compassion and stewardship.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">05</span><div><h3>Cultural and humanist influences</h3><p>Literature, film, philosophy, speculative visions of the future, and other cultural traditions have helped express enduring ideas about dignity, solidarity, reason, cooperation, nonviolence, and technology serving humanity. These influences are illustrative rather than legal, scientific, or institutional authorities.</p></div></div></div><p class="_note_y96l8_317">Detailed source inventory, methodology cautions, historical sequence and data-resource notes are maintained with the project research record.</p></section><section id="appendix-b" class="_article_y96l8_119"><p class="_articleLabel_y96l8_119">Appendix B</p><h2>Evidence and data register</h2><p>The Framework maintains an evidence architecture rather than relying on a single index or institutional source. Public claims should identify the relevant source, method, period, population, geography, and known limitations whenever these details materially affect interpretation.</p><div class="_principles_y96l8_245"><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">A</span><div><h3>Conflict and peace processes</h3><p>Conflict-event datasets, peace-agreement archives, mediation records, ceasefire documentation, and implementation monitoring are used comparatively because definitions and inclusion thresholds differ among sources.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">B</span><div><h3>Human development and public welfare</h3><p>Development, poverty, health, food, education, displacement, inequality, and livelihood indicators are used with attention to differences in collection methods, national coverage, reporting delays, and uncertainty.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">C</span><div><h3>Governance and institutions</h3><p>Administrative data, institutional records, survey evidence, perception measures, legal texts, and qualitative research may inform analysis, but no governance indicator is treated as a complete measure of a society&#x27;s legitimacy or moral worth.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">D</span><div><h3>Environment, climate, and shared resources</h3><p>Environmental and climate information should be combined with local social, economic, political, and historical evidence before drawing conclusions about conflict risk or responsibility.</p></div></div><div class="_principle_y96l8_245"><span class="_principleNumber_y96l8_258">E</span><div><h3>Evidence hierarchy and disagreement</h3><p>Primary records, transparent methods, replicable research, independent corroboration, and directly affected community knowledge should be weighed according to relevance and quality. Genuine disagreement should be documented rather than concealed.</p></div></div></div><p class="_note_y96l8_317">Current source inventories and methodology notes are maintained separately so that datasets, links, and technical references can be updated without altering the Framework&#x27;s foundational principles.</p></section><section id="declaration" class="_declaration_y96l8_203"><p class="_articleLabel_y96l8_119">Declaration</p><blockquote>Peace is not a product. People are not projects. Communities are not problems to be managed.</blockquote><p>The purpose of this Framework is to help create conditions in which people can resolve disagreements without violence, protect human dignity, strengthen their communities, expand opportunity, and sustain peace for themselves and future generations.</p></section><footer class="_footer_y96l8_346"><p>Global Peace &amp; Human Security Framework</p><p>Independent · Nonpartisan · Nonmilitary · Voluntary · Globally applicable · Locally led</p><p class="_footerSmall_y96l8_357">Release candidate RC-1, issued 29 September 2026. Subject to independent expert, legal, safeguarding, accessibility, governance, and cultural review.</p></footer></main></div></div></div><script defer src="https://floot.com/t.js"></script></body></html>